{
  "result": "At dawn, the city belongs to people who rarely appear in its advertisements. The baker is already lifting bread from the oven, and a bus driver is checking mirrors before the first passengers climb aboard. A cleaner rolls a cart down a silent hallway, turning yesterday\u2019s disorder into a fresh beginning.\n\nThese hours remind me that a place is not merely its buildings or its famous views. It is also a vast collection of small acts, performed with care, often without applause. We notice the train when it is late, but not the thousand ordinary mornings when it arrives as promised. We remember the meal, but seldom the hands that planted, packed, delivered, and prepared it.\n\nPerhaps gratitude begins with paying better attention. It does not require pretending that everything is good, or that hard work is always fairly rewarded. In fact, noticing the labor behind our comforts can make unfairness harder to ignore. It can also make kindness feel less abstract: a sincere thank you, a patient conversation, a choice to support someone else\u2019s work.\n\nBy the time the streets grow loud, the quiet workers have changed shifts, and their efforts have disappeared into the rhythm of the day. Still, they are there in the warm loaf, the clean floor, and the bus waiting at the corner. The city wakes, not all at once, but through countless people making it possible for others to begin. Tomorrow, before rushing onward, I hope to remember that, and look around more carefully.",
